    explain: "Mapping technologies to requirements means solving each one with the piece that actually solves it. \"Do not store passwords\": <b>External ID</b>, which externalises customer identity. \"Filter common web attacks\": a <b>WAF</b>. \"Do not expose secrets\": the definitive answer is not storing them better but <b>removing them</b>, with managed identity. \"Limit per client\": <b>per-subscription quotas</b> in API Management, because limiting by IP address does not identify a client. The other options fail at least one requirement: they keep custom passwords, leave an application key that is itself another secret, or confuse limiting consumption with scaling the back-end. <a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/api-management/api-management-key-concepts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Microsoft Learn ↗</a>",
